$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TYPE_DEFINITION % SEQ *!
 DEFINE_SYSTEM_DEFAULT ELEMENT 2 !
 DEFAULT_COMMAND DEF_SYS_ELEMENT VA !

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ PARAMETERS FOR LIQUID PHASE
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PHASE LIQUID:L %  1  1.0  !
  CONSTITUENT LIQUID:L :B,FE,ND,DY :  !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B;0)    298.15  +GBBLIQ;                         6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE;0)   298.15  +GFELIQ;                         6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,ND;0)   298.15  +GNDLIQ;                         1800 N !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,DY;0)   298.15  +GDYLIQ;                         3000 N !
  $ FIXED: replaced with Rong, Chen, Wang, Rao, Zhou (2017) Calphad 59, 154-163,
  $ Table 2 - genuine RK-form reassessment, fully compatible with this TDB's
  $ substitutional liquid model. No approximation needed.
  $ L0 T-term: Rong 2017 Table 2 prints -10.5074*T, this file uses +10.5074*T. With the
  $ printed sign 0 of 7 Rong Table 4 invariants are reproduced (off by 217 to 410 K). With
  $ +10.5074*T 7 of 7 are within 20 K. Cause unproven, probably a sign typo in the printed
  $ table. Not independently tested (Rong Fig. 5 activities would test it).
  $ L1 is as printed: Rong Eq. 2 uses (x_Dy - x_Fe) and pycalphad sorts DY before FE.
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE,DY;0) 298.15  -32524.7907+10.5074*T;       3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE,DY;1) 298.15  +14150.2192-1.6225*T;        3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE,DY;2) 298.15  -422.5950-6.1633*T;          3000 N ! $Rong2017
  $ REPLACED with Li, Rong, Xu, Wei, Wang, Rao, Zhou (2020) Calphad 68, 101740
  $ full RK expansion (was previously a single constant term from Van Ende
  $ Table 1, which is not directly comparable since it's an MQM pair-energy
  $ parameter, not an RK coefficient).
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,DY;0) 298.15  -81097.448+2.9271*T;          6000 N ! $Li2020
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,DY;1) 298.15  -39003.911-3.9301*T;          6000 N ! $Li2020
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,DY;2) 298.15  -10000;                       6000 N ! $Li2020
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE,ND;0) 298.15  +3221;                   6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE,ND;1) 298.15  +6216;                   6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,FE,ND;2) 298.15  +4826;                   6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,FE;0) 298.15  -133438+33.95*T;          6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,FE;1) 298.15  +7771;                    6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,FE;2) 298.15  +29739;                   6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,ND;0) 298.15  -123735+42.87*T;                  6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,ND;1) 298.15  -41869;                           6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,FE,ND;0) 298.15  +0;                            6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,ND,DY;0)    298.15  +700;                       3000 N ! $Dai2022,Table1
  $ Ternary B-Dy-Fe liquid: Yang 2025 Table 5, entered as printed (order B,DY,FE).
  $ Yang fitted it on top of the Witusiewicz Fe-B liquid. This file uses Hallstedt Fe-B,
  $ so E1 and E2 first liquid is about 1357 K against Yang 1381 K (about 25 K low).
  $ Without this term it is about 1411 K (30 K high). Scale test, diagnostic only: 0.5 gives
  $ 1387 K, 1.0 gives 1357 K. Do not scale these values.
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,DY,FE;0) 298.15  -67977.6;     6000 N ! $Yang2025,Table5
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,DY,FE;1) 298.15  +147180.8;    6000 N ! $Yang2025,Table5
  PARAMETER G(LIQUID,B,DY,FE;2) 298.15  -186500;      6000 N ! $Yang2025,Table5
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ FUNCTIONS FOR BCC_A2 PHASE
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TYPE_DEFINITION ) GES A_P_D BCC_A2 MAGNETIC  -1.0    4.00000E-01 !
 PHASE BCC_A2  %)  2 1   3 !
  CONSTITUENT BCC_A2  :B,FE,ND,DY : VA :  !
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,B:VA;0)       298.15  +GBBBCC;                    6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,FE:VA;0)      298.15  +GHSERFE;                   6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,ND:VA;0)      298.15  +GNDBCC;                    1800 N !
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,DY:VA;0)      298.15  +GDYBCC;                    3000 N !
  PARAMETER TC(BCC_A2,FE:VA;0)     298.15  +1043;              6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER BMAGN(BCC_A2,FE:VA;0)  298.15  +2.22;              6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,B,FE:VA;0)    298.15  -47920+42.09*T;     6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,FE,ND:VA;0)   298.15  +50562-4.970*T;     6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,FE,ND:VA;1)   298.15  -38667+19.10*T;     6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,B,FE,ND:VA;0) 298.15  +0;                         6000 N !
  $ ADDED: BCC_A2 Fe-Dy interaction, previously absent from this TDB.
  $ Rong et al. (2017) Table 2 gives a constant regular-solution term
  $ (no T-dependence reported).
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,FE,DY:VA;0)   298.15  +38000;                    3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(BCC_A2,ND,DY:VA;0)   298.15  +680;                      3000 N ! $Dai2022,Table1
  $ NOTE (open item, not yet fixed): DY2FE17 collapses artificially above
  $ ~800C at Fe-rich Dy-B-bearing compositions because BCC_A2 has no B-Dy
  $ term here. Yang, Wang, Liu, Wang, Zhai (2025), "Experimental
  $ determination and thermodynamic assessment of the Dy-Fe-B system",
  $ Calphad 89, 102826, Table 5, gives G(bcc,Dy:B) = GHSERDY+3*GHSERB+150000,
  $ but that paper's BCC_A2 uses a metal:interstitial(B) two-sublattice
  $ model, whereas THIS TDB's BCC_A2 has B mixing substitutionally with
  $ Fe/Nd/Dy (VA-only second sublattice) - their endmember energy is not a
  $ direct drop-in here. Needs either a properly-converted B,DY:VA
  $ interaction term or a restructure of this phase's sublattice model.
  $ Left unfixed pending professor input.
  $ STATUS: the DY2FE17 collapse described above was not re-tested as such. Alloys A3 (1173 K)
  $ and B9, B10 (1073 K) pass with this BCC model. Dy-rich and B-rich compositions untested.
  $ The Fe-B BCC and FCC terms here are Hallstedt; Yang uses Witusiewicz with an
  $ (Fe):(B,Va) site model. Swapping them is a phase-model change, not done.
 
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ PARAMETERS FOR FCC_A1 PHASE
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TYPE_DEFINITION ' GES A_P_D FCC_A1 MAGNETIC  -3.0    2.80000E-01 !
 PHASE FCC_A1  %'  2 1   1 !
  CONSTITUENT FCC_A1  :B,FE,ND,DY : VA :  !
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,B:VA;0)       298.15  +GBBFCC;                    6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,FE:VA;0)      298.15  +GFEFCC;                    6000 N !
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,ND:VA;0)      298.15  +GHSERND+5000;              1800 N !
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,DY:VA;0)      298.15  +GHSERDY+5000;              3000 N !
  PARAMETER TC(FCC_A1,FE:VA;0)     298.15  -201;               6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER BMAGN(FCC_A1,FE:VA;0)  298.15  -2.1;               6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,B,FE:VA;0)    298.15  -62952+49.90*T;     6000 N ! $94Hal1
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,FE,ND:VA;0)   298.15  +35356-2.962*T;     6000 N ! $94Hal1
  $ ADDED: FCC_A1 Fe-Dy interaction, previously absent. Rong et al. (2017)
  $ Table 2 (also used for GDYFCC lattice stability = GHSERDY + 5000,
  $ already present above at line ~175 of this file).
  PARAMETER G(FCC_A1,FE,DY:VA;0)   298.15  +38000;                    3000 N ! $Rong2017
 
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ PARAMETERS FOR RHOMBO_B PHASE
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PHASE RHOMB_B  %  1  1.0  !
  CONSTITUENT RHOMB_B  :B :  !
  PARAMETER G(RHOMB_B,B;0)  298.15  +GHSERBB;                          6000 N !
  
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ PARAMETERS FOR DHCP
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PHASE DHCP  %  1  1.0  !
    CONSTITUENT DHCP  :ND,DY:  !
   PARAMETER G(DHCP,ND;0)        298.15  +GHSERND;                     1800 N !
   PARAMETER G(DHCP,DY;0)        298.15  +GHSERDY;                     3000 N !
   PARAMETER G(DHCP,ND,DY;0)    298.15  -700;                          3000 N ! $Abe2021,0L_DHCP=-0.7kJ/mol,ab-initio-fitted
   $ NOTE: Abe et al. (2021) fit this from combined ab initio/CALPHAD analysis
   $ against phase boundary data (Kobzenko et al.). Their companion HCP_A3
   $ value is 0L=-1.6 kJ/mol, but this TDB has no HCP_A3 phase for Dy/Nd -
   $ only DHCP is present as the low-T solid solution. Adding a proper
   $ HCP_A3 solution phase (with GHSERDY/GHSERND:VA endmembers and this
   $ -1.6 kJ/mol interaction) is a separate, larger fix not done here.
   $ Liquid and BCC_A2 Dy-Nd interactions are confirmed genuinely ideal
   $ (0L=0 per Abe 2021) - the existing G(LIQUID,B,FE,ND;0)=0-style handling
   $ elsewhere in this file has no Dy-Nd LIQUID or BCC_A2 term at all, which
   $ is now understood to be correct (ideal), not a missing parameter.
   $ Yang 2025 uses HCP_A3 (Dy,B) with endmember Dy + 0.5*B + 50000. Not present here:
   $ DHCP acts as pure alpha-Dy with no B solubility. Matters only in the Dy-rich corner
   $ (Yang U15, E3, alloys B8 and A11), not for E1, E2, A1 or A2.
  
  $ HCP rare-earth solution added for the paper-aligned phase set.
  $ Dai Table 1: 0L(Dy,Nd) = -1550 + 0.61*T (298 K <= T <= 3000 K).
  $ The available unary functions are used because this hybrid TDB does not
  $ contain separate metastable HCP unary functions for both Nd and Dy.
  PHASE HCP_A3  %  1  1.0 !
   CONSTITUENT HCP_A3  :ND,DY:  !
   PARAMETER G(HCP_A3,ND;0)       298.15  +GHSERND;                    3000 N ! $engineering unary approximation
   PARAMETER G(HCP_A3,DY;0)       298.15  +GHSERDY;                    3000 N ! $engineering unary approximation
   PARAMETER G(HCP_A3,ND,DY;0)    298.15  -1550+0.61*T;               3000 N ! $Dai2022,Table1

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ PARAMETERS FOR STOICHIOMETRIC COMPOUNDS(1mol)
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 $ FeB and Fe2B: Hallstedt (94Hal1), per mole of atoms. Yang 2025 takes FeB and Fe2B from
 $ Witusiewicz 2019, written per formula unit. Not swapped. Mixing the two without
 $ re-basing the sites would be off by a factor of 2 or 3.
 PHASE FEB  %  2 0.5   0.5 !
  CONSTITUENT FEB  :FE : B :  !
  PARAMETER G(FEB,FE:B;0)  298.15 
      +0.5*GHSERFE+0.5*GHSERBB-35287+5.992*T;                  6000 N ! $94Hal1

 PHASE FE2B  %  2 0.6667  0.3333 !
  CONSTITUENT FE2B  :FE : B :  !
  PARAMETER G(FE2B,FE:B;0)  298.15 
      +0.6667*GHSERFE+0.3333*GHSERBB-26261+3.466*T;            6000 N ! $94Hal1

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ DY-B stoichiometric compounds from Van Ende et al. (2015), Table 1.
$ Gibbs energies are written per mole of atoms, consistent with this TDB.
$ DyB4 includes the +4.0 J mol^-1 K^-1 Cp correction reported in the paper.
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ DY-B: REPLACED with Li, Rong, Xu, Wei, Wang, Rao, Zhou (2020) Calphad 68,
$ 101740, via NIMS TDB (T.Abe, K.Hashimoto, Y.Goto, 2020.10.12). Genuine
$ RK-native assessment - supersedes the earlier Van Ende Table 1 transcription
$ that was confirmed broken (DyB12/DyB66 never appeared as stable phases;
$ DyB2/DyB4 were overstable). Function names aligned to this file's existing
$ GHSERBB/GHSERDY (numerically identical to NIMS's GHSERB/GHSERDY).
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 PHASE DYB2  %  2 0.6667  0.3333 !
  CONSTITUENT DYB2  :B : DY :  !
  PARAMETER G(DYB2,B:DY;0) 298.15
      -58011.6+8.521793*T+0.6667*GHSERBB+0.3333*GHSERDY;          6000 N ! $Li2020

 PHASE DYB4  %  2 0.8  0.2 !
  CONSTITUENT DYB4  :B : DY :  !
  PARAMETER G(DYB4,B:DY;0) 298.15
      -55011+6.3634*T+0.8*GHSERBB+0.2*GHSERDY;                    6000 N ! $Li2020

 PHASE DYB6  %  2 0.8571  0.1429 !
  CONSTITUENT DYB6  :B : DY :  !
  PARAMETER G(DYB6,B:DY;0) 298.15
      -40861.229+4.709*T+0.8571*GHSERBB+0.1429*GHSERDY;           6000 N ! $Li2020

 PHASE DYB12  %  2 0.9231  0.0769 !
  CONSTITUENT DYB12  :B : DY :  !
  PARAMETER G(DYB12,B:DY;0) 298.15
      -24104.7692+3.21794*T+0.9231*GHSERBB+0.0769*GHSERDY;        6000 N ! $Li2020

 PHASE DYB66  %  2 0.9851  0.0149 !
  CONSTITUENT DYB66  :B : DY :  !
  PARAMETER G(DYB66,B:DY;0) 298.15
      -5000.5+0.462333*T+0.9851*GHSERBB+0.0149*GHSERDY;           6000 N ! $Li2020

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ DYND stoichiometric compound REMOVED. Verified against the actual source:
$ Abe, Morishita, Chen, Saengdeejing, Hashimoto, Kobayashi, Ohnuma, Koyama,
$ Hirosawa (2021) Sci. Technol. Adv. Mater. 22(1), 557-570 - "Development of
$ a prototype thermodynamic database for Nd-Fe-B permanent magnets". Their
$ Dy-Nd reassessment (combined ab initio/CALPHAD) explicitly states the small
$ negative mixing energies obtained "may suggest that... a DyNd compound
$ were not stable in this system." No stoichiometric DYND phase belongs in
$ this TDB. (They introduce a narrow-range peritectoid reaction near
$ Dy-50at%Nd instead of a compound - not implemented here as it requires
$ phase-boundary-fit parameters beyond scope of this fix.)
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ [History: v5 fixed T-term signs, DYFE2 formula and TC in K. The liquid L1 value now in this file is correct, see the note at the Dy-Fe liquid.]
$ Dy-Fe compounds: REPLACED with Rong, Chen, Wang, Rao, Zhou (2017)
$ Calphad 59, 154-163, Table 2 - genuine RK/CEF reassessment.
$ NOTE: the values these replace (Konar-sourced, via Van Ende 2015) had a
$ real unit bug - TC was written in Celsius (e.g. 371) instead of Kelvin
$ (644.15), which would have put every magnetic transition ~273K off.
$ Fe2Dy's exact low-temperature (<120K) piecewise Cp anomaly from Rong's
$ Eq. 10 was not transcribed here (OCR of that equation was not reliable
$ enough to trust); Fe2Dy below uses Neumann-Kopp + TC/BMAGN as an
$ approximation, consistent with how Fe14Dy2B was handled elsewhere in
$ this file. Fe17Dy2, Fe23Dy6, and Fe3Dy ARE exact transcriptions of
$ Rong's stated formulas (Neumann-Kopp is explicitly correct for these
$ three per the paper's Eqs. 12-14).
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 $ DY-FE COMPOUND CHECKS (this block replaces older comments that wrongly suggested a
 $ DyFe2 transcription error):
 $ - Every Dy-Fe compound parameter matches Rong 2017 Table 2 as printed. No DyFe2
 $   error exists. Do not exclude DYFE2.
 $ - Compounds are written in formula-unit form (sites 1:2, 1:3, 6:23, 2:17) so that
 $   pycalphad divides the magnetic term by the correct N (3, 4, 29, 19).
 $ - 298 K formation enthalpies match Rong within 0.07 kJ/atom for all four compounds.
 $ - Residual limitation: DYFE2 uses Neumann-Kopp plus TC/BMAGN, not Rong's piecewise
 $   Eq. 10 Cp, so the shape of the magnetic Cp peak near 600 K is approximate.
 PHASE DYFE2  %&  2 1 2 !
  CONSTITUENT DYFE2 :DY : FE : !
  PARAMETER TC(DYFE2,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +635.0;                     3000 N ! $Rong2017,Kfixed
  PARAMETER BMAGN(DYFE2,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +5.75;                    3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(DYFE2,DY:FE;0) 298.15
      GHSERDY+2*GHSERFE+(-45100+26.48*T-T*LN(T)-8E-3*T**2-100000*T**(-1)+3E-7*T**3);                3000 N ! $Rong2017,S298=39.43J/mol/K-matched,dH298-corrected

 PHASE DYFE3  %&  2 1 3 !
  CONSTITUENT DYFE3 :DY : FE : !
  PARAMETER TC(DYFE3,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +606.0;                     3000 N ! $Rong2017,Kfixed
  PARAMETER BMAGN(DYFE3,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +3.97;                    3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(DYFE3,DY:FE;0) 298.15
      GHSERDY+3*GHSERFE-62000+13.1662*T;                 3000 N ! $Rong2017

 PHASE DY6FE23  %&  2 6 23 !
  CONSTITUENT DY6FE23 :DY : FE : !
  PARAMETER TC(DY6FE23,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +534.0;                   3000 N ! $Rong2017,Kfixed
  PARAMETER BMAGN(DY6FE23,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +14.9;                  3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(DY6FE23,DY:FE;0) 298.15
      6*GHSERDY+23*GHSERFE-475000+132.5*T;       3000 N ! $Rong2017

 PHASE DY2FE17  %&  2 2 17 !
  CONSTITUENT DY2FE17 :DY : FE : !
  PARAMETER TC(DY2FE17,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +371.0;                   3000 N ! $Rong2017,Kfixed
  PARAMETER BMAGN(DY2FE17,DY:FE;0) 298.15 +16.1;                  3000 N ! $Rong2017
  PARAMETER G(DY2FE17,DY:FE;0) 298.15
      2*GHSERDY+17*GHSERFE-250000+80.0448*T;       3000 N ! $Rong2017

 TYPE_DEFINITION & GES A_P_D FE17ND2 MAGNETIC  -3.0    2.80000E-01 !
 PHASE FE17ND2  %&  2 0.8947   0.1053 !
    CONSTITUENT FE17ND2  :FE : ND :  !
   PARAMETER TC(FE17ND2,FE:ND;0)     298.15  +327.15;          6000 N ! $94Hal2
   PARAMETER BMAGN(FE17ND2,FE:ND;0)  298.15  +2.3;             6000 N ! $94Hal2
   PARAMETER G(FE17ND2,FE:ND;0)      298.15
   -7385+3.600*T+0.8947*GHSERFE+0.1053*GHSERND;                6000 N ! $94Hal2
   
 TYPE_DEFINITION " GES A_P_D FE17ND5 MAGNETIC  -3.0    2.80000E-01 !
 PHASE FE17ND5  %"  2 0.7727  0.2273 !
    CONSTITUENT FE17ND5  :FE :ND :  !
   PARAMETER TC(FE17ND5,FE:ND;0)     298.15  +503.15;          6000 N ! $94Hal2
   PARAMETER BMAGN(FE17ND5,FE:ND;0)  298.15  +2.3;             6000 N ! $94Hal2
   PARAMETER G(FE17ND5,FE:ND;0)  298.15
    -6175+2.698*T+0.7727*GHSERFE+0.2273*GHSERND;               6000 N ! $94Hal2
    
 PHASE ND2B5  %  2   0.7143  0.2857 !
  CONSTITUENT ND2B5  :B : ND :  !
  PARAMETER G(ND2B5,B:ND;0)  298.15 
     -63259+14.19*T+0.2857*GHSERND+0.7143*GHSERBB;                     6000 N !

 PHASE NDB4  %  2   0.8  0.2 !
  CONSTITUENT NDB4  :B : ND :  !
  PARAMETER G(NDB4,B:ND;0)  298.15 
     -71066+16.62*T+0.2*GHSERND+0.8*GHSERBB;                           6000 N !

 PHASE NDB6  %  2   0.8571  0.1429 !
  CONSTITUENT NDB6  :B : ND :  !
  PARAMETER G(NDB6,B:ND;0)  298.15 
     -50910+8.978*T+0.1429*GHSERND+0.8571*GHSERBB;                     6000 N !

 PHASE NDB66  %  2   0.9851  0.0149 !
  CONSTITUENT NDB66  :B : ND :  !
  PARAMETER G(NDB66,B:ND;0)  298.15 
     -7226+1.133*T+0.0149*GHSERND+0.9851*GHSERBB;                      6000 N !

 TYPE_DEFINITION ( GES A_P_D FE14ND2B MAGNETIC  -3.0    2.80000E-01 !
 PHASE FE14ND2B  %(  3  0.0588  0.8235  0.1177 !
    CONSTITUENT FE14ND2B  :B : FE : ND,DY :  !
   PARAMETER TC(FE14ND2B,B:FE:ND;0)     298.15  +586.15;               6000 N !
   PARAMETER BMAGN(FE14ND2B,B:FE:ND;0)  298.15  +2.4;                  6000 N !
   PARAMETER G(FE14ND2B,B:FE:ND;0)      298.15
   -11312+2.813*T+0.0588*GHSERBB+0.8235*GHSERFE+0.1177*GHSERND;        6000 N !
   PARAMETER G(FE14ND2B,B:FE:ND,DY;0) 298.15 +0;                 3000 N ! $VanEnde2015,L=0(ideal)
   $ G(FE14ND2B,B:FE:DY;0) is defined further below, sourced from Table 1
   $ TC/BMAGN for the DY endmember: Van Ende et al. (2015) report a measured
   $ Curie temperature of 592 K for T1-Dy, and state it has "similar magnetic
   $ behaviour" to T1-Nd - used as justification for reusing BMAGN=2.4 below.
   $ This activates the existing Inden-Hillert magnetic model (already wired
   $ via this phase's TYPE_DEFINITION) to reproduce the Curie anomaly, instead
   $ of relying on the smooth Neumann-Kopp-only endmember G function.
   PARAMETER TC(FE14ND2B,B:FE:DY;0)     298.15  +594.1;               3000 N ! $Yang2025,Tc(was 592 VanEnde2015)
   PARAMETER BMAGN(FE14ND2B,B:FE:DY;0)  298.15  +0.59;                 3000 N ! $Yang2025,muB-per-atom(was 2.4 VanEnde2015)
   $ OPEN: Yang 2025 uses Tc 594.1 K and 0.59 muB/atom for Dy2Fe14B and a ferro-para
   $ gap of about 0.941 kJ/mol at Tc. This file still has 592 K and 2.4. T1 sites sum to 1
   $ here, so whether the magnetic term needs the formula-unit fix used for the Dy-Fe
   $ compounds (2:14:1) is untested. Magnetism is negligible above about 700 K.

 TYPE_DEFINITION 1 GES A_P_D FE4NDB4 MAGNETIC  -3.0    2.80000E-01 !
 PHASE FE4NDB4  %1  3 0.43956044  0.43956044  0.12087912 !
    CONSTITUENT FE4NDB4  :B : FE : ND,DY :  !
   PARAMETER TC(FE4NDB4,B:FE:ND;0)     298.15  +10.15;                 6000 N !
   PARAMETER BMAGN(FE4NDB4,B:FE:ND;0)  298.15  +0.2;                   6000 N !
   $ T2 Fe4Nd1.1B4: Cp reconstructed from Van Ende & Jung (2013), Table 1.
   $ Residual G is per mole of atoms and is added to the exact stoichiometric
   $ reference-state combination (4 Fe + 4 B + 1.1 Nd)/9.1.
   $ Source Cp: 4.44 Cp(Fe2B) + 1.11 Cp(NdB4) - 4.89 Cp(FeB).
   $ Formation data: DeltaH298=-262567 J/mol-formula; DeltaS_f,298=56.33 J/mol-formula/K.
   PARAMETER G(FE4NDB4,B:FE:ND;0) 298.15 +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   +1.005507974E-05*T**3-3.439182390E-02*T**2+2.246353407E+01*T*LN(T)
   -1.400307652E+02*T-2.395553639E+04-1.080336451E+05*T**(-1); 400 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   +7.931050550E-08*T**3+6.452774725E-05*T**2+3.840483516E-01*T*LN(T)
   -6.431776959*T-3.015741983E+04+2.132077458E+05*T**(-1); 900 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   -1.127004736E-06*T**3+6.233608791E-03*T**2-4.702723678*T*LN(T)
   +2.521356686E+01*T-3.173017902E+04+3.179733173E+05*T**(-1); 1100 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   -8.437352857E-07*T**3+3.567048352E-03*T**2+1.063312670E-01*T*LN(T)
   -8.935831964*T-2.781185267E+04-2.874891003E+05*T**(-1); 1128 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   +3.713709890E-08*T**3-2.534808791E-03*T**2+7.305320879*T*LN(T)
   -5.640642842E+01*T-2.474847769E+04-3.880375619E+05*T**(-1); 1800 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   -1.462781923E-06*T**3+1.632605448E-02*T**2-3.572387388E+01*T*LN(T)
   +2.582296652E+02*T-6.722566137E+04+7.386859625E+06*T**(-1); 1811 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   -1.488683857E-06*T**3+1.439307866E-02*T**2-2.584004970E+01*T*LN(T)
   +1.812281032E+02*T-5.552927409E+04+7.420863361E+06*T**(-1)
   -1.009244176E+31*T**(-9); 2348 Y
   +0.43956044*GHSERBB+0.43956044*GHSERFE+0.12087912*GHSERND
   -1.488683857E-06*T**3+1.439307866E-02*T**2-2.584004970E+01*T*LN(T)
   +1.812281032E+02*T-5.552927409E+04+7.420863361E+06*T**(-1)
   -1.009244176E+31*T**(-9); 3000 N ! $VanEndeJung2013
 $ Dai et al. (2022), Table 1: Dy1.1Fe4B4 endmember for tau2.
 $ This is put on the same per-mole-of-atoms basis as FE4NDB4.
 PARAMETER G(FE4NDB4,B:FE:DY;0) 298.15
     -50410+11*T+0.1208440*GHSERDY+0.4390780*GFEFCC+0.4390780*GHSERBB; 3000 N ! $Dai2022,Table1

 $ ADDED: T2 Dy-endmember (Fe4DyB4), previously missing from this TDB
 $ entirely (FE4NDB4's rare-earth sublattice only allowed ND). Added here
 $ as a separate stoichiometric phase rather than extending FE4NDB4 to
 $ (ND,DY), matching how the source paper itself models it (no Nd-Dy
 $ solid solution on this compound).
 $ Source: Yang, Wang, Liu, Wang, Zhai (2025), "Experimental determination
 $ and thermodynamic assessment of the Dy-Fe-B system", Calphad 89, 102826,
 $ Table 5 (their tau3 = DyFe4B4): G = -50674 + 10.6750*T + (1/9)*GHSERDY
 $ + (4/9)*GHSERFE + (4/9)*GHSERB. No TC/BMAGN reported for this compound
 $ in the source paper, so treated as non-magnetic (Neumann-Kopp only),
 $ consistent with their treatment.
 $ Site fractions written as 0.444444444 / 0.111111111 so they sum to 1 (were 0.4444 and
 $ 0.1111, which sum to 0.9999). Effect on energies is under 2 J/atom.
 $ Separate DYFE4B4 phase folded into FE4NDB4 tau2 solution above.

 TYPE_DEFINITION ~  GES A_P_D FE2ND5B6 MAGNETIC  -3.0    2.80000E-01 !
 PHASE FE2ND5B6  %~  3  0.4615  0.1539  0.3846 !
    CONSTITUENT FE2ND5B6  :B : FE : ND :  !
   PARAMETER TC(FE2ND5B6,B:FE:ND;0)     298.15  +64.15;                6000 N !
   PARAMETER BMAGN(FE2ND5B6,B:FE:ND;0)  298.15  +0.5;                  6000 N !
   PARAMETER G(FE2ND5B6,B:FE:ND;0)      298.15
   -42341+7.572*T+0.4615*GHSERBB+0.1539*GHSERFE+0.3846*GHSERND;        6000 N !

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ (Old Van Ende Table1 DYB2-DYB66 block REMOVED - it was a duplicate PHASE
$ definition left over from an earlier patch, silently double-counted by
$ pycalphad alongside the correct Li et al. 2020 block above, which caused
$ DYB66's G to come out wildly wrong. The Li 2020 block above is now the
$ only definition for these five phases.)
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ REPLACED: FE14ND2B B:FE:DY endmember, previously a hand-fit piecewise
$ polynomial with breakpoints at 450/592/616K that coincided suspiciously
$ with the declared Curie temperature (TC=592K below), raising a risk of
$ double-counting the magnetic contribution alongside the Inden-Hillert
$ model already active on this phase.
$
$ Now sourced from a dedicated Dy-Fe-B ternary assessment (tau1-Dy2Fe14B).
$ Source table gave three ranges (T<50, 50-398, >398K); the T<50 piece is
$ omitted here since this parameter's valid range starts at 298.15K,
$ matching every other parameter in this file - only the 298.15-398 and
$ >398 pieces are used. These ranges are a standard low/high-T Debye-style
$ Cp split with no breakpoint near 592K - so they should NOT already
$ contain the Curie anomaly, and the TC/BMAGN parameters below remain the
$ sole source of magnetic contribution, added once via the model.
$
$ NOTE: this table reports G directly on the SER scale (no explicit
$ +x*GHSERDY+y*GHSERFE+z*GHSERBB terms), unlike this file's Nd endmember
$ and unlike tau3/tau4/tau5/tau6 in the same source table. If comparing
$ this endmember's formation energy against DYFE4B4 or others later,
$ subtract 0.1177*GHSERDY+0.8235*GHSERFE+0.0588*GHSERBB from this G first
$ to put it on the same "relative to elements" basis.
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ T1-Dy endmember (FE14ND2B,B:FE:DY): Yang et al. 2025, Table 5, tau1-Dy2Fe14B, both
$ pieces (298.15-398 K and above 398 K). History: in DNFB_6 and DNFB_7 a Dai et al. (2022)
$ piece (Materials 15, Table 1) replaced the Yang piece above 800 K. It was more stable
$ than Yang's by 4.5 kJ/atom at 800 K, falling to 0.3 kJ/atom at 1500 K, which made T1
$ too stable (alloys A7, B1, B2 failed). DNFB_8 removed the Dai piece: A7, B1, B2 now pass
$ and nothing else broke. Do not restore the Dai piece. The older idea that the Yang piece
$ causes Dy to be rejected from T1 was wrong. (Dai mapping, if ever needed again:
$ 0G_Dy^hcp -> GHSERDY, 0G_Fe^fcc -> GFEFCC, 0G_B^rho -> GHSERBB.)
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   PARAMETER G(FE14ND2B,B:FE:DY;0)      298.15
   -20328.4576-14.0389*T+5.7563*T*LN(T)-0.1343*T**2+1.2805E-04*T**3
   +1888.8526*T**(-1)-6.3704E-08*T**4;                                398 Y
   -17199+58.6628*T-12.5284*T*LN(T)-1.7884E-02*T**2+3.4991E-06*T**3
   -303036.9072*T**(-1)-4.3491E-10*T**4;   3000 N ! $Yang2025 Table5 T>398K, no upper limit in paper, Fig7 shows Cp smooth to 2000K, untested above

$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ NOTES ON THIS HYBRID EXTENSION
$ 1) The uploaded base database uses a substitutional single-sublattice LIQUID.
$    The 2015 paper uses the Modified Quasichemical Model for Dy-B and adopts
$    the Konar MCM description for Dy-Fe. Therefore the Dy-Fe and Dy-B liquid
$    terms above retain only the leading binary interaction term so the file
$    remains compatible with the uploaded phase model.
$ 2) The solid Dy-B, Dy-Nd, Dy-Fe and T1 endmember parameters are taken from
$    the literature values; T1 Dy/Nd mixing is treated as ideal (L_NdDy=0).
$    CONFIRMED against Van Ende et al. (2015) Table 1: this is not a missing
$    fit on our part - the original paper's own optimization deliberately
$    treated Fe14(Nd,Dy)2B as an ideal solution (0L_NdDy = 0 stated directly
$    in their results). Open question is whether "ideal" remains adequate
$    for this quaternary's actual composition range, not whether a value is
$    missing.
$ 3) GDYBCC verified identical to SGTE/Dinsdale GBCCDY (unary50.tdb, NIMS) -
$    confirmed matching coefficients across all 4 temperature ranges. This is
$    the real assessed lattice-stability function, not an approximation.
$ 4) OPEN for the quaternary: T1-Dy is Yang 2025 (SER-scale polynomial) while T1-Nd
$    (untagged in this file) is a formation-energy form from another source. The Nd/Dy
$    preference in a mixed T1 depends on their difference, so both endmembers must come
$    from the same source and basis. Untested. T1 Nd-Dy mixing is still ideal.
$ 5) Fe-B source differs from Yang 2025 (Hallstedt 94Hal1 here, Witusiewicz 2019 there).
$    Changing the Fe-B liquid, BCC, FCC, FEB or FE2B later means retesting Nd-Fe-B too.
$------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$------------------------------------------------------------------ END OF LINE
 
